{\cs117\super #}{ direct_address_location}}K{\footnote \pard\plain \s121\ql \li120\ri0\sl-220\slmult0\widctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin120\itap0 \f1\fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\cs117\super K}{ address location;address location,direct;
}}}{\fs20 Direct Address Location
\par }\pard\plain \ql \li120\ri0\sa60\sl-220\slmult0\widctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin120\itap0 \f1\fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {{\*\bkmkend WHAT_Topic}
Direct address location selects a graphic entity based on a street address associated with that entity. Typically the entity will be a parcel area or parcel centroid. The user must supply an attribute file containing stree
t addresses for the graphic entities to be selected. This file must be a GeoMaster data file (.GMD suffix) with an index containing the street name, house number and ZIP code. The graphic entity must be TAGed with the UDI portion of the TAG contained in t
he attribute file. The entities must be contained in a single graphics layer and there must be a TAG index built on this layer which includes the TAG of the entities to be selected. 
\par 
\par The file }{\b [%DATA_LOC]addloc.txt }{configures the direct address location. It
 specifies the pathname of the address location table, the prefix of the TAG used to locate the graphic entity and the data presented to the user when entering addresses to be located. Use the addloc.txt file in the GeoMaster installation directory as a t
emplate.
\par 
\par To create the GMD file you should use the data file utility }{\b Create Address Location Table}{. This utility allows you to use any attribute file containing the street address information to create the address location table. 
\par }}